{"qid":"Q784","names":["Commonwealth of Dominica"],"title":"Commonwealth of Dominica","endonym":{"native":"Commonwealth of Dominica","lang":"en"},"capsule":"The Commonwealth of Dominica is a small island republic in the Lesser Antilles of the eastern Caribbean, the most mountainous island in the region and home to the last significant Kalinago (Island Carib) population in the Americas. Britain granted independence on 3 November 1978, and Dominica became a republic immediately without a monarchical transitional phase, unlike most other Commonwealth Caribbean states. Its early years were turbulent: two coup attempts occurred in 1981, and Prime Minister Eugenia Charles — the Caribbean's first female head of government — governed from 1980 to 1995, supporting the 1983 US intervention in Grenada. Dominica's economy relies on bananas, eco-tourism, and more recently citizenship-by-investment revenues.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. As a contemporary sovereign state, Dominica's territorial extent and political status are precisely documented; the attestation window reflects its post-independence existence with full confidence.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Dominica","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Dominica"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Dominica","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dominica"}],"britannica":"place/Dominica","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Dominica","rulers":[{"qid":"Q1281003","name":"Patrick John","start":1978,"end":1979,"image":null},{"qid":"Q324474","name":"Oliver Seraphin","start":1979,"end":1980,"image":null},{"qid":"Q258685","name":"Eugenia Charles","start":1980,"end":1995,